libf — software floating point
==============================

WORK IN PROGRESS.  ccc has no native float/double type, so FLOAT is an
unsigned long holding the bits of a 1-sign / 7-exponent (bias 64) /
24-mantissa value (the leading 1 in bit 23 is explicit).  See float.h
for the format and the interface; float.c is the arithmetic, mathf.c
the elementary functions.  fbcd.s and finc.s are the older assembly
helpers, kept here but no longer called by the C code.

The archive is built for both variants like libc and libu: libf.a
(Z80) and libf280.a (Z280).  float.c and mathf.c are compiled with
-I.. because ccc's preprocessor searches the include path, not the
source directory, so "float.h" would otherwise resolve to the empty
stub in include/.

Known defect
------------

fatof parses a fraction digit-by-digit as d * tenth, where tenth is
0.1 held inexactly in this format.  fatof("3.5") therefore yields
3.4999... rather than exactly 3.5, and ftoa truncates instead of
rounding, so ftoa(fatof("3.5"), 2) prints "3.49".

The fix is to accumulate the fraction as an integer (parse "3.5" as
35/10, not 3 + 5*0.1) or to round to nearest in ftoa; not yet done.
